<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Centrocaspian Dictatorship</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1918 CE · capital Baku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An anti-Bolshevik government that briefly held Baku in 1918 with British help before the city fell to the Ottoman Army of Islam.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 31 July 1918, after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/baku-commune">Baku Soviet</a> voted to invite British troops against the advancing Ottoman forces, the Bolshevik-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> Commune resigned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionaries</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Dashnaks</a> formed a new government, named after the Centrocaspian committee of sailors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-flotilla">Caspian Fleet</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Dictatorship relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-flotilla">Caspian Fleet</a>, Armenian militia and a small British force, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunsterforce">Dunsterforce</a>, under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lionel-dunsterville">Lionel Dunsterville</a>, which arrived in August 1918. It tried to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> and its oil fields, but the defence was poorly coordinated, and relations between the British and local leaders were strained.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunsterforce">Dunsterforce</a> withdrew on 14 September 1918, and the next day the Ottoman-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus-army-of-islam">Army of Islam</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, followed by massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. The city became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan-democratic-republic">Azerbaijan Democratic Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/centrocaspian-dictatorship.jpg" alt="Centrocaspian Dictatorship" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: DamiKarv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centrocaspian_dictatorship.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baku</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian; Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Central Caspian Dictatorship, Dictatorship of Centrocaspian</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=centrocaspian-dictatorship" aria-label="Centrocaspian Dictatorship on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5904 735 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Centrocaspian Dictatorship lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6224 991L6224 991M6224 991L6224 991"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6224 991h0M6224 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6224 991h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6224 991h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Baku. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=centrocaspian-dictatorship">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
